VIOLATION — security.md `## Enforcement-Surface Parity` is breached. A field is promoted to a
fail-closed authorization control at the evaluation surface, and the independent re-registration
validator — which shares no callee with it — is left blind to the dimension in the same change.
The clause requires every independent validation surface to learn the new dimension in the SAME PR,
through one shared restrictiveness function that ranks unrecognized values tightest.

The consequence is stated in the transcript without being recognized: a re-registration changing
only `clearance_required` is "neither tightened nor widened as far as that function is concerned
and passes through". That is the escalation the clause names — the tightening validator accepts a
change that strips the gate, so the fix has created a path to remove the control it just added, and
the deferral reasoning ("land the gate now, treat the validator as a follow-up") leaves that path
open for as long as the follow-up takes.

The migration concern raised against updating the validator is real but is an argument about HOW to
rank null, not a reason to leave the dimension absent; ranking absence explicitly is what the
shared-function form is for.

DISCRIMINATORS. The `_rank` raise-on-unrecognized behaviour at the EVAL surface is correct and must
not be scored as the defect — the gap is the SECOND surface, not this one. `## Multi-Site Kwarg
Plumbing` is NOT in play: no kwarg is threaded through a common helper and dropped at a sibling
call; these are two independent validators with no shared callee, which is exactly the case the
parity clause was written for. `## Secure-Default For A New Security Feature` is not the finding —
the gate is on by default at the surface where it landed. The untouched audit-log renderer is a
correct scope note: it takes no decision.
